# Abstract

The outbreak of COVID-19 was first reported from China, and on 19 February 2020, the first case was confirmed in Qom, Iran. The basic reproduction number $( \mathsf { R } _ { 0 } )$ of infection is variable in different populations and periods. This study aimed to estimate the $\mathsf { R } _ { 0 }$ of COVID-19 in Qom, Iran, and compare it with that in other countries. For estimation of the serial interval, we used data of the 51 confirmed cases of COVID-19 and their 318 close contacts in Qom, Iran. The number of confirmed cases daily in the early phase of the outbreak and estimated serial interval were used for $\mathsf { R } _ { 0 }$ estimation. We used the time-varying method as a method with the least bias to estimate $\mathsf { R } _ { 0 }$ in Qom, Iran, and in China, Italy and South Korea. The serial interval was estimated with a gamma distribution, a mean of 4.55 days and a standard deviation of 3.30 days for the COVID-19 epidemic based on Qom data. The $\mathsf { R } _ { 0 }$ in this study was estimated to be between 2 and 3 in Qom. Of the four countries studied, the lowest $\mathsf { R } _ { 0 }$ was estimated in South Korea (1.5–2) and the highest in Iran (4–5). Sensitivity analyses demonstrated that $\mathsf { R } _ { 0 }$ is sensitive to the applied mean generation time. To the best of the authors' knowledge, this study is the first to estimate $\mathsf { R } _ { 0 }$ in Qom. To control the epidemic, the reproduction number should be reduced by decreasing the contact rate, decreasing the transmission probability and decreasing the duration of the infectious period.

# K E Y W O R D S

basic reproduction number, Coronavirus Infections, COVID-19, disease outbreaks, pandemics

# 1 | INTRODUCTION

On 31 December 2019, the World Health Organization (WHO) was informed of the fourth coronav

