020,2020/03/13,PMC7107560,,10.1093/jtm/taaa033,,COVID-19; Novel coronavirus disease; early outbreak; reproduction number; serial interval; statistical analysis,title_only,lenient,666.5,2263.5,2930.0,82474.6,strong_candidate,S,4,"The paper explicitly investigates the serial interval of COVID-19 to estimate the reproduction number during an early outbreak. Although the specific geographic setting is not detailed in the sparse metadata, the direct alignment with the target parameter and disease makes it a strong candidate for full-text verification.",4,"Title and keywords explicitly mention COVID-19 and novel coronavirus disease. Evidence is limited to metadata, but disease focus is unmistakable.",3,"Study of outbreak dynamics and reproduction numbers inherently involves human populations, though not explicitly stated in the sparse metadata.",2,"Mentions 'early outbreak' suggesting a real-world setting, but no specific geographic location is provided in the limited title/keywords.",3,"Keywords indicate 'statistical analysis' and parameter estimation, suggesting primary empirical or modeling work rather than a review, based on available metadata.",4,Title and keywords explicitly state 'serial interval' as a core focus for estimating the reproduction number.,0.9,,,,"# Serial interval in determining the estimation of reproduction number of the novel coronavirus disease (COVID-19) during the early outbreak

