# Statistical Analysis

Categorical variables were presented as frequency (percentage) and continuous variables were reported as arithmetic mean and standard deviation. Group comparisons were performed using Chi-square $( \chi ^ { 2 } )$ test or Fisher’s exact test. Exposure time, isolation time, and diagnosis time were compared among groups using the Kruskal-Wallis test, followed by Tukey’s test using ranks for multiple comparisons. R version 3.6.2 was used and two-sided $p$ values less than 0.05 were considered significant.

We estimated median incubation time and important quantiles (2.5th and 97.5th percentiles) along with bootstrapped confidence intervals (CIs) following a log-normal distribution (Reich, Lessler, Cummings, & Brookmeyer, 2009). We fitted a gamma distribution to the transmission pairs data to estimate the serial interval (SI) distribution, as

a substantial proportion of transmission probably occurred before the first symptoms in the index case (He et al., 2020). The time-dependent reproductive number $( R _ { t } )$ of the cluster and the city was calculated using the method developed by Thompson et al. (Thompson et al., 2019) . The daily number of reported COVID-19 cases and the SI derived from a previous epidemiological survey of the first 425 cases in Wuhan (Q. Li et al., 2020) were used to estimate $R _ { t }$ and its $9 5 \%$ CI on each day via a 5-day moving average.

Comparative analysis of family and social clusters was conducted according to the time interval of illness onset and isolation between the first generation, second generation, and third generation. The timeliness and complexity of different cluster infections were also analyzed.

