# Clinical Characteristics

Regarding clinical symptoms, two cases were asymptomatic infectors $( 3 . 5 \% )$ and 55 were acute onset cases, mainly manifested as fever (37 cases) with a body temperature (armpit) of $3 7 . 4 \mathrm { - } 3 9 . 3 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ lasting for 5–8 days. Thirteen cases had cough and chills, and some had cough (26 cases), fatigue (six cases), chills (four cases), headache (three cases), and other symptoms (13 cases). Computed tomography (CT) of the chest showed that there were 31 cases of ground-glass pneumonia. There were no deaths in the sample. The 57 patients tested positive for nucleic acid by RT-PCR, and included four cases of severe pneumonia, 27 cases of ordinary pneumonia, and 26 cases of mild illness or asymptomatic infection. Except for the first case, the severe cases in the next two generations were among people aged 70, 91, and 84 years. Table 1 shows the tendency of cases to be asymptomatic or mild as the infection is transmitted from the first to third generations.

