33088292,Meta-analysis of several epidemic characteristics of COVID-19.,"Zhang P, Wang T, Xie SX.",J Data Sci. 2020 Jul;18(3):536-549. doi: 10.6339/JDS.202007_18(3).0019.,Zhang,Journal of data science : JDS,2020,2020/10/22,PMC7575205,NIHMS1636611,10.6339/JDS.202007_18(3).0019,"As the COVID-19 pandemic has strongly disrupted people's daily work and life, a great amount of scientific research has been conducted to understand the key characteristics of this new epidemic. In this manuscript, we focus on four crucial epidemic metrics with regard to the COVID-19, namely the basic reproduction number, the incubation period, the serial interval and the epidemic doubling time. We collect relevant studies based on the COVID-19 data in China and conduct a meta-analysis to obtain pooled estimates on the four metrics. From the summary results, we conclude that the COVID-19 has stronger transmissibility than SARS, implying that stringent public health strategies are necessary.",basic reproduction number; epidemic doubling time; incubation period; meta-analysis; sensitivity analysis; serial interval,,title_abstract,strict,1155.0,2171.5,3326.5,81385.2,possible_candidate,P,3,"The paper directly addresses the serial interval of COVID-19 through a meta-analysis of existing studies in China. However, it lacks original primary data collection, relying instead on secondary synthesis of published estimates.",4,Explicitly investigates COVID-19 epidemic characteristics.,4,Focuses on human COVID-19 cases and transmission metrics.,3,"Based on COVID-19 data collected from China, providing a real-world geographic context.",1,This is a meta-analysis synthesizing existing studies rather than reporting primary empirical data collection.,2,"Reports pooled estimates for the serial interval derived from other studies, not from the authors' own individual-level contact tracing data.",0.8,,,,33088292,,Meta-analysis of several epidemic characteristics of COVID-19.,"Zhang P, Wang T, Xie SX.",J Data Sci. 