ases experienced by this region in a very short timeframe. The slight decrease of the reproduction number observed in the early days after February 20, 2020 might be due to increased population awareness and early interventions implemented before the regional lockdown imposed on March 8, 2020.",COVID-19; Coronavirus; Epidemiology; Lombardy outbreak; Reproduction number; SARS-CoV-2; Transmission dynamics,title_abstract,strict,1662.0,1725.0,3387.0,63793.2,strong_candidate,S,4,"This paper directly addresses the research question by analyzing human COVID-19 case data in Italy and explicitly reporting a mean serial interval estimate. All dimensions are strongly supported by the abstract, making it a strong candidate for inclusion.",4,Explicitly investigates SARS-CoV-2 and COVID-19 epidemiology.,4,Analyzes human case records and close contacts in Lombardy.,4,"Set in Lombardy, Italy, providing a clear real-world geographic context.",4,Reports original empirical analysis of a consolidated line list of laboratory-confirmed cases and their contacts.,4,Explicitly reports an estimate of the mean serial interval (6.6 days) derived directly from the study's own observed data.,1.0,,,,"Title: The early phase of the COVID-19 epidemic in Lombardy, Italy.

