# 4. Serial interval distribution

Data on close contacts of positive cases were collected through standardized interviews performed by the ATS operators of the Lombardy Region. For each case and contact, operators collected data on their demographic characteristics (such as name, gender, date of birth, home address, municipality of residence), clinical symptoms (such as the list of symptoms experienced and the date of symptom onset) as well as the history of exposure (epidemiological link between a positive case and his/her close contacts). Data storage, anonymization, and management were carried out by local health authorities. Confidential information was removed before conducting the analysis. We estimated the serial intervals for pairs of primary infectors and secondary cases between cases in the same cluster, connected by an epidemiological link using the anonymized time lags between the date of symptom onset of each infector and those reported by their positive symptomatic contacts.

Of the 6,106 reported contacts, only $3 . 8 \%$ resulted in a SARS-CoV-2 infection. Of these 233, for 90 pairs of cases we found an infector-infectee relationship and have the dates of symptom onset of both cases. Identified secondary cases were ascribed to 55 unique primary cases.

A gamma distribution was fitted to the obtained set of individual serial intervals by using maximum likelihood estimation. The best fitting gamma distribution (Fig. S1) has mean 6.6 days and median 5.5 days (shape 1.87, SD 0.26; scale 0.28, SD 0.04).

![](images/2cf9ee703a44b98ddcef60b06f6a86b6777eebea6368da8ca4589dff91af67f4.jpg)  
Figure S1. Red bars represent observed lags between the symptom onset dates of secondary cases and their corresponding primary infectors. The solid line and the shaded area show the estimated mean and 95%CI of the serial interval as obtained by the best-fitting gamma distribution.

# 5. Estimation of the reproduction number

The basic reproduction number $R _ { 0 }$ represents the average number of secondary cases generated by a primary infector in a fully susceptible population. In general terms, when $R _ { 0 }$ is larger than 1 the infection can spread in the population and the larger $R o$ the larger effort is required to control the epidemic. However, when the spread of a disease is affected by different factors (such as a decline of the number of susceptible individuals, the enactment of control interventions, or a change in

individuals’ behavioral responses), its transmission potential at a given time t is better measured in terms of the net reproduction number $R t$ . The net reproduction number is useful to track the effectiveness of performed control measures and other factors affecting the spread of the epidemic (e.g., the behavioral response of the population) over time. As soon as $R t$ falls below 1, the epidemic starts to decline.

To estimate $R t$ , we use the same methodology presented in reference [9-11]. We assumed that the daily number of new cases (date of symptom onset) with locally acquired infection L(t) can be approximated by a Poisson distribution according to the equation

$$
L (t) \sim \mathrm {P o i s} \left(R t \sum_ {s = 0} ^ {t} \varphi (s) C (t - s)\right)
$$

where

• C(t), with t from 1 to T, is daily number of new cases (date of symptom onset);   
• $R t$ is the net reproduction number at time t;   
$\varphi ( s )$ is the distribution of the generation time calculated at time s. $\varphi ( s )$ was approximated by using the distribution of the serial interval as estimated from the data following the approach described in section 4.

The likelihood $\mathcal { L }$ of the observed time series of cases from day 1 to day T conditional on C(0) is thus given by

$$
\mathcal {L} = \prod_ {t = 1} ^ {T} P \left(L (t); R t \sum_ {s = 1} ^ {t} \varphi (s) C (t - s))\right)
$$

where $\mathrm { P } ( \mathrm { k } ; \lambda )$ is the probability mass function of a Poisson distribution (i.e., the probability of observing k events if these events occur with rate $\lambda$ ).

We then used MCMC Metropolis-Hastings sampling to estimate the posterior distribution of Rt. To estimate ${ \mathrm { R } } _ { 0 }$ , we assumed that and used the above-described procedure. To apply the above-described procedure for the computation of the basic reproduction number within each province of Lombardy region, we identified an initial phase of exponential growth by visual inspection of the curve of symptomatic cases. Then, we assumed that during the period where the ep

